# Line endings and file types for CERNAL.
#
# Why this file exists at all:
# The Git for Windows installer sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide. Until this file
# existed, that setting won by default: every text file was stored LF in the repository
# but written CRLF into a Windows working copy. For most files that is harmless. For the
# `do` task runner it is fatal — a script whose shebang line ends in CRLF makes Linux read
# the interpreter name as "bash\r", and WSL answers:
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
# which is exit code 127 and means nothing to anyone who has not seen it before.
# Git Bash happens to tolerate it; WSL does not. So the documented WSL setup was broken
# for exactly the people it was written for.
#
# A fresh `git clone` made after this file is committed already gets it right.
# An EXISTING Windows checkout does not: the files are stored LF but were already written
# to disk as CRLF, and Git will not rewrite them just because the rules changed. Check with
#     git ls-files --eol do
# If that prints `w/crlf`, commit or stash your work first, then force a re-checkout:
#     git rm --cached -r .
#     git reset --hard
# Anything that then shows as modified is a line-ending fix, not a content change.

# Let Git decide what is text and normalise it to LF in the repository. Text files still
# arrive in your working copy with whatever endings your platform and core.autocrlf want —
# this line only guarantees that what is *stored* is LF, so the repository never carries
# CRLF for one person and LF for another.
* text=auto

# --- Files that must be LF in the working copy too, not just in the repository ---------
# These carry a `#!` shebang and are executed directly (mode 100755). The kernel reads the
# shebang byte for byte, so a trailing \r becomes part of the interpreter's name.

do          text eol=lf
manage.py   text eol=lf

# Catalog provenance hashes the shipped CSV bytes. Keep their recorded LF bytes
# on checkout, including when Git for Windows enables core.autocrlf=true.
src/apps/expression/catalog/**/*.csv text eol=lf

# --- The barrier run: LF in the working copy too --------------------------------------
#
# docs/assets/barrier/README.md records a SHA-256 per file so a reader can tell the
# tracked rows from a local re-run. `* text=auto` stores LF and checks out CRLF on
# Windows, which would make every one of those digests fail on a Windows clone while the
# repository is perfectly intact -- the same failure the expression catalog below hit,
# where 3,001 CRLF made an undamaged file hash wrong. Pinning the checkout is what makes
# a recorded digest mean the same thing on every machine.
docs/assets/barrier/*.csv text eol=lf

# --- Binary: never touch these, and never try to merge them ---------------------------
# Without this, an unlucky auto-detection could rewrite bytes inside an image or a PDF.
*.png       binary
*.pdf       binary

# --- Generated lockfiles ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Cosmetic only: this collapses them by default in GitHub pull-request diffs so a
# 30,000-line regenerated lock does not bury the twenty lines of science you actually
# want reviewed. They are still tracked, still diffable locally, and still must be
# regenerated with `uv lock` / `npm install` — never hand-edited, and never resolved by
# hand during a merge conflict.
uv.lock                     linguist-generated=true
frontend/package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true

# vendor/: third-party code used UNMODIFIED, so Git must not touch a single byte.
#
# `* text=auto` above normalises line endings to LF in the repository. For our own files
# that is what we want. For vendored files it silently rewrites them: the AIS-China
# library ships CRLF, and storing it as LF makes the committed bytes differ from upstream.
# That is not cosmetic. Their own config/rna_model.json records
# energy_parameter_sha256 = f4a71a54...  over the CRLF bytes of config/rna_turner2004.par,
# and the LF version hashes to 9521527a... So normalisation would:
#   * break tests/engine/test_ais_china_energy_model.py on a fresh clone,
#   * break VENDORED_TREE_SHA256,
#   * and make the "no modifications" statement that Apache-2.0 4(b) asks for untrue —
#     line-ending conversion is a modification.
# `-text` means "never infer text, never convert": the bytes go in and come out unchanged.
vendor/ais-china-codon-optimization-v2/** -text

# --- The pinned expression catalog: LF in the working copy too -----------------------
#
# Same failure mode as vendor/ above, one layer in. Each dataset's
# provider_metadata.csv_sha256 in catalog/manifest.json is recorded over the STORED
# bytes, and tests/test_catalog_governance.py reads the file back with read_bytes() and
# compares. `* text=auto` stores LF and checks out CRLF on Windows, so the comparison
# fails on every Windows clone while the repository is perfectly intact. Measured across
# all 15 entries: the git blob matches the recorded digest every time, the on-disk copy
# carries 3,001 CRLF and does not, and LF-normalising the on-disk bytes reproduces the
# recorded digest every time.
#
# `text eol=lf` rather than vendor's `-text`: these are text that the catalog pipeline
# regenerates and someone reviews, so diff and merge handling should stay on. They are
# already stored as LF, so nothing committed changes -- only the checkout stops being
# rewritten, which is what makes the digest mean the same thing on every platform.
src/apps/expression/catalog/**/*.csv  text eol=lf
